---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../data/site";
---
<Layout title="About">
<main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 sm:py-16 lg:py-20">
<a href="/" class="inline-block mb-8 text-sm text-gray-500 hover:text-white transition">← Home</a>
<p class="lab-label mb-4">Identity / Engineering Lab</p>
<h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">About the Lab</h1>
<p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-400">{SITE.tagline}</p>
<article class="prose prose-invert mt-10">
<h2>What this is</h2>
<p>Alok Engineering Lab is a personal engineering laboratory and digital garden. It is a place to build systems, test ideas, document decisions and preserve useful technical knowledge over time.</p>
<h2>What I explore</h2>
<p>My work moves across software architecture, automation, AI systems, developer tooling, Linux and system experimentation, information systems and small prototypes.</p>
<h2>How I build</h2>
<p>I prefer small, inspectable systems over complexity added for appearance. I want important decisions to have evidence behind them, failures to become lessons, and architecture to remain understandable as projects grow.</p>
<h2>Why keep a Lab?</h2>
<p>A portfolio usually shows outcomes. An engineering lab can preserve the process: constraints, failed approaches, experiments, trade-offs, verification and changing ideas. That history is often more valuable than a polished final screenshot.</p>
<h2>Current direction</h2>
<p>The Lab is evolving from a project archive into a connected engineering knowledge system. The goal is not to publish everything, but to preserve the work and reasoning worth revisiting.</p>
</article>
</main>
</Layout>
